Let $f_1,\ldots,f_n$ be a homogeneous regular sequence in $S=\mathbb C[x_1,\ldots,x_n]$, with $\deg f_i=a_i>0$. Suppose that a field extension $k/\mathbb C$ and a finite-dimensional central division $k$-algebra $\Delta$ have the following property. There is a finite collection of pairwise commuting elements of $\Delta[x_1,\ldots,x_n]_1$, containing $x_1,\ldots,x_n$, whose generated commutative $k$-algebra $A$ contains elements $t_1,\ldots,t_n$ of degree one satisfying $$\label{eq:triangular}
 t_i^{a_i}
 =c_i f_i+\sum_{l<i}B_{il}f_l+\sum_{d>i}C_{id}t_d,
 \qquad 1\le i\le n,$$ where $c_i\in k^\times$ and all terms are homogeneous, with $B_{il},C_{id}\in A$. Then $A$ is finite over $k[x_1,\ldots,x_n]$, and $$\{t_1^{\alpha_1}\cdots t_n^{\alpha_n}:\alpha\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^n\}$$ is a basis of $\Delta[x_1,\ldots,x_n]$ as a left $\Delta$-vector space.

For the data of Theorem <a href="#thm:division" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:division">18</a> and a right $\Delta$-module of reduced rank $r$, there is a class $\beta$ of $H$-equivariant coherent sheaves on $Z\times X$, with a commuting right $\Delta_0$-action, having the following properties.*



*After any embedding $k\hookrightarrow\mathbb C$, identify $\Delta_0\otimes_k\mathbb C$ with $\operatorname{Mat}_{e_0}(\mathbb C)$, choose a primitive matrix idempotent $\epsilon$, and put $\beta'=\beta\epsilon$. The class $\beta'$ is still $H$-equivariant and is perfect on $Z\times X_{\mathrm{sm}}$. For every $x\in X_{\mathrm{sm}}(\mathbb C)$, $$\label{eq:descent-fiber-index}
\int_Z\mathop{\mathrm{ch}}\bigl(\beta'|_{Z\times x}\bigr)\mathop{\mathrm{td}}(Z)=\pm r.$$ The sign depends only on the curve factors, not on the module or on $x$. Before making the complex base change, one has $$\label{eq:descent-twist-divisibility}
p(l):=\frac{\chi(Z\times X,\beta\otimes\mathcal O_X(l))}{e_0}
       \ \in\ e_0e_1\mathbb Z\qquad(l\in\mathbb Z).$$ After complex base change, $p(l)$ is also the ordinary Euler characteristic of $\beta'\otimes\mathcal O_X(l)$.

Let $V$ be a smooth complex projective variety and let $\xi\in K^0(V\times\mathbb P^h(\mathbb C))$. There is a unique $I_V(\xi)\in K^0(\mathbb P^h(\mathbb C))$ such that $$\label{eq:index-integral-pushforward}
 \mathop{\mathrm{ch}}(I_V(\xi))
   =\int_V\mathop{\mathrm{ch}}(\xi)\mathop{\mathrm{td}}(T_V).$$ The integral on the right is integration over the $V$-factor.
